<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Feather is still the better fit for most experienced club play and many league fixtures. Nylon is usually the sensible choice for beginners, schools and sessions where durability matters most. But the interesting part is everything hidden inside that simple answer.</strong></p>

      <p>A tube of feather shuttles can cost several times as much as a tube of nylon. That makes the buying decision look obvious until you notice that the tubes often contain different numbers of shuttles, the products fail in completely different ways, league rules vary by division, and the newest synthetic shuttles are no longer confined to leisure-centre practice courts.</p>

      <p>So this is not a debate about whether feather is "better". It is a question of <strong>what you are buying the shuttle for, what standard your players expect, and what each playable hour actually costs your club.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The practical answer</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>League fixtures:</strong> use whatever the current rules for your division require. Do not assume.</li>
        <li><strong>Experienced club nights:</strong> feather is usually preferred where players are accustomed to it and the budget supports it.</li>
        <li><strong>Beginners, schools and high-volume coaching:</strong> nylon is usually the more practical default.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed-purpose clubs:</strong> there is nothing wrong with stocking more than one type.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> compare price per shuttle and then measure how many your club actually retires per session.</li>
        <li><strong>Synthetic feather:</strong> treat it as a separate emerging category, not as another name for ordinary nylon.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Prices and product ranges were checked in the UK on 26 August 2026. They move quickly, so the examples below are there to show the arithmetic rather than provide a permanent price list.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#difference">What actually feels different?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Which is cheaper in practice?</a>
